> **WARNING:**
> In the flake-parts ecosystem, `perSystem` is **never** an output. It is always a function (or attribute set) provided as an argument to `flake-parts.lib.mkFlake`. All outputs like `devShells`, `packages`, etc. are produced by flake-parts by calling your `perSystem` function for each system and collecting the results under the appropriate output attributes. You should **never** use, produce, or access a `perSystem` output. If you see code like `outputs.perSystem.x86_64-linux`, it is incorrect and should be refactored.
>
> Example from the [flake-parts template](mdc:https:/github.com/hercules-ci/flake-parts/blob/main/template/default/flake.nix):
> ```nix
> outputs = inputs@{ flake-parts, ... }:
>   flake-parts.lib.mkFlake { inherit inputs; } {
>     systems = [ "x86_64-linux" ];
>     perSystem = { config, self', inputs', pkgs, system, ... }: {
>       packages.default = pkgs.hello;
>     };
>   };
> ```
> As shown, `perSystem` is a function argument, not an output.
>
> To verify this in upstream code, use gitingest:
>   - To view the template: `gitingest hercules-ci/flake-parts template/default/flake.nix`
>   - To view the README: `gitingest hercules-ci/flake-parts README.md`
> Look for how `perSystem` is used: always as a function argument, never as an output.

## Running Tests

To run the nix-unit tests for the current system (recommended):

```sh
nix build .#checks.$(nix eval --raw --impure --expr builtins.currentSystem).nix-unit -L
```

This will build and run the nix-unit test suite for your current system.
